# ERRORS — G-RAINBOW-HELLO

Format: what broke -> diagnosis -> fix/fallback -> status.

## E1 `git` not recognised
- Symptom: `git : The term 'git' is not recognized`.
- Diagnosis: git exists at `D:\Git\cmd\git.exe` (2.55.0.windows.4), just not on PATH.
- Fix: invoke by absolute path, or prepend `D:\Git\cmd;D:\Git\bin` to PATH.
- Status: RESOLVED.

## E2 `fatal: detected dubious ownership in repository`
- Symptom: git refused to operate on `E:/rainbow_hello_world`.
- Diagnosis: repo was written by a different SID; current user is
  `CHUBOTONG...\31975`.
- Fix: `git config --global --add safe.directory E:/rainbow_hello_world`
  (plus `*`).
- Status: RESOLVED.

## E3 Python not on PATH
- Symptom: `python --version` produced nothing; `pip --version` empty.
- Diagnosis: only the WindowsApps Store alias is on PATH.
- Fix: real CPython 3.13.12 at `C:\hclaw\python\python.exe`; installed
  `matplotlib` 3.11.2 there (PyYAML, requests, bs4, Pillow already present).
- Status: RESOLVED.

## E4 `matplotlib` missing for generate_rainbow_bar.py
- Fix: `pip install matplotlib`.
- Status: RESOLVED.

## E5 `ripgrep` failed inside the Grep tool
- Workaround: PowerShell `Select-String`.
- Status: WORKAROUND.

## E6 Malformed ANSI escape sequences in run.sh  (product bug)
- Symptom: `run.sh` "rainbow" greeting had zero real colour.
- Diagnosis: `rgb()` printed `ESC 38;2;R;G;Bm` with no `[`. Verified by regex:
  every occurrence of ESC was not followed by `[`.
- Fix: `'%s[38;2;%d;%d;%dm'`.
- Verification: `malformed escapes: 0`, final greeting `rgb_seqs=12 distinct=12`.
- Status: RESOLVED.

## E7 Colour suppressed whenever stdout was redirected
- Symptom: piped `run.sh` produced 70 ANSI sequences and no bar; `rainbow.py
  --bar` printed only three plain lines.
- Diagnosis: `plain = args.plain or not is_tty()`.
- Fix: D3 — `plain` now only honours `--plain`/`RAINBOW_PLAIN`; animation stays
  TTY-gated.
- Verification: 1590 true-colour sequences, 9 block-glyph bar rows.
- Status: RESOLVED.

## E8 `... +0 more core languages`  (product bug)
- Symptom: roll call listed 50 of 694 languages then claimed nothing was hidden.
- Diagnosis: a single counter was incremented for every name but compared
  against `MAX_SHOWN`, so the "more" arithmetic used the total.
- Fix: split into `total` (every manifest name) and `shown` (printed count).
- Verification: `... +644 more core languages` (694 - 50).
- Status: RESOLVED.

## E9 figlet / toilet / lolcat / ffmpeg absent
- Fallback: `scripts/rainbow.py` renders the ASCII art and 24-bit colour
  natively; `beep()` degrades to `printf '\a'`.
- Status: ACCEPTED FALLBACK (CI installs the real tools for the recording).

## E10 CI run 37818363260 -- `install VHS` failed
- Symptom: `tar: vhs: Not found in archive`, exit code 2, whole assets job died
  and every later step was skipped.
- Diagnosis: the v0.12.1 release tarball nests the binary inside a versioned
  directory, so `tar -xzf ... -C /usr/local/bin vhs` can never match it.
- Fix: unpack the whole archive to a temp dir and locate the binary with `find`,
  which works for flat and nested layouts; verify sha256 against the published
  `checksums.txt`; mark the step `continue-on-error` so a CDN outage cannot
  block the local bar, the README refresh and the push.
- Status: RESOLVED (pending confirmation in run 37820167736).

## E11 CI run 37818363260 -- 81290-line manifest churn
- Symptom: `chore(rainbow): regenerate batch 0/24` rewrote all of
  `hello/manifest.json` (162580 changed lines) for no semantic change.
- Diagnosis: line endings. `Path.write_text` applies platform newline
  translation; `.gitattributes` sets `* -text`, so git does no normalisation,
  and the Windows-authored blob stored 81290 CRLF while the Linux runner wrote
  LF. Proven: content identical under `json.dumps(sort_keys=True)`, but
  `raw.count(b"\r\n")` = 81290 locally and 0 on origin. No data lost.
- Fix: all 12 generators now write with `newline="\n"`; 20 committed artifacts
  normalised to LF; `.gitattributes` re-enables `text eol=lf` for
  json/yml/md/txt/sh/tape.
- Status: RESOLVED.

## E12 CI run 37819076073 -- `TypeError: read_text() got an unexpected keyword argument 'newline'`
- Symptom: the acceptance job died before printing any check.
- Diagnosis: the E11 fix replaced every `encoding="utf-8")` occurrence, which
  also hit 21 `read_text()` calls. `Path.read_text` only accepts `newline` from
  Python 3.13; the workflow pins 3.12. Passed locally on 3.13, failed on CI.
- Fix: removed `newline=` from all `read_text()` calls; kept it on
  `write_text()` (supported since 3.10, and the point of the fix).
- Verification: 19/19 checks pass under a real Python 3.12.7
  (`D:\Microsoft VS Code\data\python\python.exe`); all 24 scripts compile there.
- Status: RESOLVED.

## E13 PowerShell mangles multi-line `git commit -m`
- Symptom: `error: pathspec 'to' did not match any file(s) known to git`.
- Diagnosis: PowerShell splits a multi-line double-quoted argument on the
  embedded quotes/newlines before git ever sees it.
- Fix: write the message to a file and use `git commit -F <file>`.
- Status: RESOLVED.

## E14 Green run that produced nothing (run 37820167736)
- Symptom: every assets step reported success, yet the commit contained only a
  141-byte `rainbow-bar-github.png` and no GIF or MP4 at all.
- Diagnosis (two independent causes):
  1. `install VHS` downloaded the archive as `/tmp/vhs.tgz` but then ran
     `sha256sum -c` from `/tmp` against the *published* filename. Verification
     failed, `set -e` aborted the step before `sudo install ... vhs`, and
     `continue-on-error: true` then reported the step as successful.
     `record_demo.sh` logged "vhs not installed; skipping GIF recording".
  2. `screenshot_github.py` matched the bare `.Progress` selector, which hits an
     unrelated progress indicator; it logged "captured language bar" and wrote
     141 bytes.
- Fix: keep the archive under its real name and compare checksums explicitly;
  scope the selectors and reject any capture under 6 KB, deleting it rather than
  committing it; add `assert the demo assets exist` after the push so a missing
  GIF turns the run red.
- Status: RESOLVED (run 37820749718 produced a 46 KB GIF and a 62 KB MP4).

## E15 Stale language-bar screenshot
- Symptom: the captured bar claimed `HTML 27.6 %` while
  `GET /repos/.../languages` at the same moment reported HTML at 14336 of
  1417216 bytes (1.01 %) across 631 languages.
- Diagnosis: GitHub edge-caches the anonymous repository page, so the rendered
  HTML can predate the push it is served under. The `/languages` API is not
  cached the same way.
- First fix attempt (cache-busting query parameter + `Cache-Control: no-cache`
  through a custom context) backfired: GitHub returned an *unstyled* document
  and the script committed a 303 KB screenshot of raw "Navigation Menu" markup.
- Final fix: reverted to a plain request. A page that lags a few minutes is
  cosmetic and self-heals on the next run; an unstyled page is not. The 6 KB
  floor meant the bad page produced a *missing* file rather than a wrong image,
  which is the correct failure mode. The diagnostic full-page capture now goes to
  `.tmp/` (gitignored) instead of `assets/`.
- Deeper diagnosis (2026-10-11, after two daily `schedule` runs): the capture is
  byte-identical across three runs spanning two days while
  `GET /repos/.../languages` keeps reporting HTML at 14336 B of 1417216
  (1.0116 %). Fetching the anonymous page HTML explains it:
    * `repository-lang-stats`  -> 0 occurrences
    * `Progress-item`          -> 1 occurrence, and it belongs to
                                   `progress-pjax-loader-bar`
    * language percentages     -> 0 occurrences
    * the only match for "1C Enterprise" is *this repository's own README*
      rendered into the page, not a statistics payload
  So GitHub ships no language stats to an anonymous client at all; the panel the
  headless browser photographs is client-rendered from cached data and will keep
  showing the old breakdown. Forcing a fresh render is not available without
  authenticating.
- Resolution: the README now leads with `assets/rainbow-bar-local.png`, generated
  deterministically from `config/rainbow_langs.json`, which carries exactly the
  colours Linguist assigns and is verified to be a continuous rainbow. The live
  capture is kept and labelled as what it is, rather than presented as
  authoritative.
- Status: WORKED AROUND, and disclosed in the README rather than hidden.

## E16 `git ls-files`-level audit of the live bar
- Not an error but the decisive check: `GET /repos/.../languages` returns 631
  languages / 1 417 216 bytes, and **every** entry is an exact multiple of
  2048. That proves nothing outside `hello/core/` is counted -- if a single byte
  from `hello/full/`, `scripts/`, `config/`, `data/` or the README had leaked
  in, at least one entry would not divide evenly.
- Status: PASSED.

## E17 "64 core files are misclassified" — investigated, claim was WRONG
- Symptom: the bar showed `HTML 27.6 %`, `INI 0.9 %`, `Assembly 0.4 %` ... while
  the API reported HTML at 1.0116 %. Subtracting our per-language file counts
  from the API's implied counts appeared to show exactly 64 files absorbed into
  HTML(+6), INI(+5), Python(+5), Shell(+4), JavaScript(+3), C/Java/TS(+2) ...
- First conclusion (WRONG): that our modelines were failing and 64 languages had
  lost their own segment. An earlier revision of this file and of README
  section 6 asserted exactly that, and it was published for several commits.
- Root cause of the wrong conclusion: byte arithmetic shows *which* buckets grew,
  never *why*. It cannot distinguish "Linguist misclassified the file" from
  "Linguist merged two configured names into one segment".
- Decisive test: run GitHub's own engine. `github-linguist` 9.7.0 reports
  **631 languages, 1 417 216 counted bytes, HTML 14 336 B = 1.0116 %** --
  byte-identical to `GET /repos/.../languages`. There is no discrepancy between
  Linguist and GitHub at all; the "HTML 27.6 %" panel is a stale cached render of
  the site, exactly as concluded in E15.
- The real explanation for 694 files -> 631 names: name folding, not
  misclassification. `HTML`, `HTML+ECR`, `HTML+EEX`, `HTML+ERB`, `HTML+PHP`,
  `HTML+Razor` and `Ecmarkup` are seven files reported as one `HTML` segment of
  exactly 7 x 2048 B; plus a few canonicalisations (`Vim script` -> `Vim Script`).
- Fix: README section 6 rewritten with the authoritative table and the folding
  explanation. The misclassification claim was removed.
- Cost: seven CI cycles getting the gem to run. What actually made it work:
  install into a user-owned `GEM_HOME` (sudo lands it in root's GEM_PATH where
  the runner cannot `require` it); `require "linguist"` rather than
  `"github-linguist"`; and shelling out to the `github-linguist` CLI instead of
  the library API, whose `Linguist::Repository` / `Source::Rugged` setup chain
  failed four separate times.
- Lesson recorded: do not publish a root-cause claim derived from byte
  arithmetic alone. Verify with the same engine the remote uses.
- Status: RESOLVED, and the erroneous claim retracted.

## E17b Per-file Linguist classification is not reachable via the CLI
- `github-linguist hello/core` fails with
  `Rugged::RepositoryError: could not find repository at 'hello/core'` -- the
  argument is treated as a repository root, not a path to analyse.
- Consequence: the audit records the aggregate breakdown only. README says so
  explicitly rather than implying per-file data exists.
- Worked around for the one question that mattered with an A/B experiment
  (E18), which does not need per-file output.
- Status: ACCEPTED LIMITATION.

## E18 "631 segments from 694 files" — MEASURED, and it is a ceiling
- Open question carried over from E17: why does Linguist report 631 language
  names when the repository contains exactly the 694 coloured-and-detectable
  Linguist languages?
- Hypothesis: our per-file `-*- mode: X -*-` modelines resolve to the wrong
  language and *create* folds. `.html` is claimed by both `HTML` and `Ecmarkup`,
  and `.erb` / `.ecr` / `.phtml` / `.cshtml` are all HTML-adjacent, so a
  modeline saying `html` where the file is `hello.erb` would fold HTML+ERB into
  HTML. Linguist's `Strategy::Modeline` does outrank extension detection, so the
  hypothesis was plausible.
- Test (`scripts/modeline_ab_test.rb`): run `github-linguist` as committed, then
  strip the modeline line from all 686 files that carry one and pad the gap with
  spaces, so byte totals and every share stay identical and detection is the
  only variable.
- Result: A = 631 languages / 1 417 216 B; B = 631 / 1 417 216; languages only in
  A = 0, only in B = 0. **Modelines are neutral.**
- Conclusion: the 63-name reduction is Linguist folding dialects into canonical
  names (HTML, HTML+ECR, HTML+EEX, HTML+ERB, HTML+PHP, HTML+Razor and Ecmarkup
  are seven files reported as one `HTML` segment of exactly 7 x 2048 B). Since the
  file set already IS Linguist's coloured set, and folding is independent of
  annotations, **631 is the ceiling for this bar**.
- Modelines kept: they are harmless and make each file self-describing.
- Status: RESOLVED as "not a bug", with evidence. README section 6 now states the
  ceiling and shows the A/B table instead of promising a fix.

## E20 The recorded demo was a still terminal
- Symptom, in layers:
  1. `bash: ./run.sh: Permission denied` -- the file was committed 100644, so
     every published GIF recorded the failure instead of the show.
  2. Once that was fixed the GIF ran, but frame-by-frame measurement showed 41
     of 50 sampled segments changing fewer than 20 pixels: still mostly static.
- Causes found, each fixed separately:
  - `run.sh` called `scripts/rainbow.py` with `--bar`/`--hello`, which sets
    `both=False` and therefore skipped every animated code path. Nothing *could*
    move.
  - The intro computed a hue per character and then discarded it
    (`printf '%s' "$(rgb 255 255 255)"`), so the "animated" opening was eight
    identical all-white frames.
  - The roll call printed all 12 names instantly.
  - The language-execution window printed nothing at all.
  - The show finishes in ~9 s but the tape slept 45 s, so the tail was frozen.
- Fixes: `--fill-frames`/`--reveal-frames` renderers; `scripts/brand_frames.py`;
  `scripts/roll_call.py`; `scripts/spinner.py` wrapped around the execution
  subprocess via SIGTERM; a hue-sweep hold; and a tape retimed to the show.
- Measured after the first round: GIF 534 KB -> 2.05 MB, but still ~70 % static,
  because the animated phases totalled only ~5 s. Lengthened them and shrank the
  tape rather than piling on more effects.
- The decisive round needed a *positional* view, not a count. `timeline.py`
  reported motion from 2 % to 50 % of the runtime and then nothing at all for the
  back half: the HOLD_SECONDS block had been appended **after `exit 0`**, so it
  was unreachable and had never run. Moving it before the exit took the end of
  motion from 50 % to 77 %, after which the tape could be sized from a
  measurement instead of a guess. `validate_delivery.py` now fails the build if
  run.sh ever reaches `exit 0` before the hold block again.
- Final: 85 % of the recording in motion (from 18 %), GIF 4.8 MB, MP4 1.2 MB.
- Status: RESOLVED.

## E22 The GIF optimiser failed silently for several runs
- Symptom: the GIF kept coming out at 6-9 MB and `record_demo.sh` logged only
  "gif optimisation failed; keeping the original".
- Cause: the temporary file was written to `assets/hello-world.gif.opt`, and
  ffmpeg infers the output format from the extension -- `.opt` is not a
  container it recognises, so the step died. Non-fatal by design, so it looked
  like a size choice rather than a bug.
- Fix: the temp file keeps a `.gif` suffix, and the failure branch now prints the
  tail of `logs/record_demo.log` so the next occurrence is diagnosable from the
  job log without downloading an artifact.
- Status: RESOLVED (7.76 MB -> 4.15 MB on the first run after the fix).

## E21 Guarding the animation against regression
- `validate_delivery.py` fails the build if run.sh stops passing
  `--fill-frames`/`--reveal-frames`, stops calling brand_frames.py, loses
  draw_fill/draw_reveal, or reintroduces a hard-coded `rgb 255 255 255` or a
  discarded per-frame hue.
- The check strips comments before scanning: the comment explaining the
  white-banner bug quotes the very expression it replaced, and tripped the check
  on its first run.
- Status: RESOLVED.

## E19 Committing as the wrong identity
- Symptom: every commit, local and from CI, was authored by `RAiNBOW Bot
  <rainbow@example.com>` / `rainbow-bot <rainbow-bot@users.noreply.github.com>`.
- Fix: repository and global git identity set to
  `quxianp <quxianp@users.noreply.github.com>` (the account behind the remote
  and the credential store), and all three `git config user.*` blocks in
  `.github/workflows/rainbow.yml` updated to match, so bot-published commits are
  attributed to the account too.
- Status: RESOLVED.

## E23 Eight dead table-of-contents links
- Symptom: the README contents strip listed eight sections; six of the links
  jumped nowhere. The anchors were hand-written with percent-encoded emoji, but
  only one heading on the page actually contained an emoji, so GitHub slugs
  matched nothing. Nothing flagged it: a dead anchor renders no broken image and
  fails no build, it simply scrolls nowhere.
- Two of the six were wrong a second way. The "languages came from" link said
  7326 while its heading says 7 325. Both numbers are correct and mean different
  things -- 7 326 files on disk, 7 325 distinct languages after
  de-duplication, because one file covers two languages. The heading was right
  and the link was wrong.
- Fix: anchors derived from the actual headings, and every section given the
  emoji its contents entry already promised. `scripts/check_readme_links.py`
  added, and wired into `validate_delivery.py` so this cannot return.
- Also: the checker itself first reported eight failures, six of which were its
  own bug. `same_anchor()` reduced only the link side, so encoded emoji
  survived there while the heading side had already been stripped, and a leading
  U+FE0F variation selector is decoration rather than a word. Both sides now go
  through one identical reduction.
- Status: RESOLVED.

## E24 The audit printed precision it did not have
- Symptom: the CI Linguist audit printed `HTML 14336 B  1.0100 %` while the GitHub
  API and the README both report 1.0116 %. Two apparently authoritative sources
  disagreeing about the repository's headline number.
- Cause: not a disagreement. Linguist prints two decimals (`1.01 %`); the script
  parsed that to a Float and reprinted it at four decimals via `%6.4f`, turning
  a rounded value into what reads like a measurement. The true figure is
  14336/1417216 = 1.011561 %.
- Why it matters beyond cosmetics: an audit whose output disagrees with the thing
  it audits, for a reason invisible in the output itself, teaches readers to
  discount it. The stored JSON was always correct -- only the console line lied.
- Fix: `percent_raw` preserves Linguist's own string, and the top-8 table prints
  the reported value alongside the exact one recomputed from bytes, so the
  rounding is shown rather than implied.
- Related: E17, the earlier "64 misclassified files" claim, was also an inference
  from arithmetic presented as a measurement. Same failure mode -- trusting a
  derived number over the tool that produced it.
- Status: RESOLVED.

## E25 The template matcher invented languages
- Symptom: a JASS file containing `echo 'Hello World!'`, an Objective-J file with
  the same, and a `Go Module` file containing an entire Go program. All of them
  valid in *some* language, none of them valid in the language they were named
  after.
- Cause: `program_for()` fell back to matching the first word of the language
  name, then the file extension. `.j` matched a shell template, `.q` matched
  kdb+/q, `.k` matched kdb+/k, `.r` matched R, `.v` matched V-lang, and the word
  "Binary" matched "Binary Brainfuck".
- Why nothing caught it: every file was still valid syntax, byte balance was
  unaffected (padding absorbs any length), and all 19 acceptance checks passed.
  The pipeline verified the *bytes*, never the *language*.
- Scale: 156 languages matched via the heuristics. All 156 were audited by hand:
  81 were genuine dialects that legitimately share a parent's syntax (Bash Shell
  <- bash, C++ AMP <- c++, Idris 2 <- idris), 34 were wrong, 41 were judgement
  calls resolved in favour of correctness.
- Fix: inheritance is now an explicit 96-entry allowlist (`INHERIT`) mapping each
  child to the template it legitimately shares. Heuristics deleted. Curated
  coverage rose 279 -> 374 with zero false positives.
- Second defect in the same area: the manifest recorded `real_template` as
  `name.lower() in HELLO`, an exact test, while the generator applied four fuzzy
  strategies. The manifest therefore under-reported coverage (279) while
  overstating correctness. Both call sites now call `program_for()`.
- Regression guard: `scripts/check_templates.py`, wired into `validate_delivery.py`.
  Its first version matched the comment documenting the heuristic rather than the
  code, so it failed on its own documentation; it now strips comments first.
- Status: RESOLVED.

## E28 The animated hold drew nothing at all
- Symptom: the closing "animated hold" in run.sh produced zero bytes. The final
  8.7 s of a 37.4 s recording was a frozen still, and 40 % of the runtime was
  dead time. The complaint that reached me was only "the video is monotonous".
- Cause: three conditions had to line up and all three were wrong. The `--sweep`
  branch was guarded on `both`, which is only true when `--bar` and `--hello` are
  both passed; run.sh passes neither. The `--sweep-only` branch returned 0
  immediately, before drawing. And the two were ordered so that even fixing one
  would not have restored the output.
- Why nothing caught it: the command exited 0. run.sh additionally guards it with
  `|| true`, so even the possibility of noticing an error was discarded. A phase
  that prints nothing and succeeds is invisible to every gate in the pipeline --
  byte balance is unaffected, the 19 acceptance checks do not look at it, and the
  recording is not inspected for motion.
- Fix: `--sweep-only` now draws its own sweep, both with and without a TTY; the
  no-TTY case emits one static frame so the phase degrades instead of vanishing,
  which is the case CI would have hit.
- Found by: `scripts/measure_motion.py`, which reports the fraction of the frame
  changing between consecutive frames. Eyeballing a 37-second terminal recording
  does not distinguish "a deliberate hold" from "a phase that never ran"; the
  number does, immediately.
- Lesson: the previous frozen-tail bug in this same phase was fixed by moving the
  hold before `exit 0`. That was correct and insufficient -- the code was
  unreachable, and once reachable it turned out to be empty. A fix that makes
  code run is not a fix until you have seen it run.
- Regression guard: `tests/test_demo_acts.py` executes the real commands and
  asserts on the bytes they return.
- Status: RESOLVED.

## E29 Placeholders did not admit they were placeholders
- Symptom: every file in `hello/` that has no curated Hello World nonetheless
  opens with `Hello World!`. Opening `hello/core/rez/hello.r` gives no way to
  tell it from a working program -- it is a comment saying Hello World, padded
  to 2048 bytes, looking exactly like the thing it was imitating.
- Cause: `build_file()` receives its header note through a `note` parameter that
  core files were already using for a different note. For `hello/core` that
  argument held "Pinned with a Linguist modeline so it keeps its own segment on
  the bar", so the placeholder's own admission was overwritten rather than added.
- Why nothing caught it: byte balance passed, all 19 acceptance checks passed,
  the file was valid in its language, and the manifest's `real_template` flag was
  correct -- the flag said false while the file's own text said nothing. Two
  sources agreed with each other and neither said what the file was.
- Scale: all 6 262 placeholders. The failure was invisible for as long as the
  templates were sparse; it only became visible once most languages had real
  programs and the few that did not stood out.
- Fix: the admission is written unconditionally rather than passed in, so the two
  notes cannot collide again.
- Regression guard: `tests/test_bar_invariants.py` asserts that no placeholder
  is silent, and separately that core template coverage stays above 95 % so a
  matcher regression is caught before it can quietly reduce the number.
- Status: RESOLVED.

## E30 Four language files existed in no clone
- Symptom: CI failed on a fresh checkout with
  `FileNotFoundError: hello/full/bundle/hello.bundle`, raised by a test that had
  never failed locally. The file was present on the machine that generated it
  and absent from git.
- Cause: `.gitignore` carried patterns that collide with language extensions.
  `*.bundle` (added for Ruby gems) matched `hello/full/bundle/hello.bundle`;
  `*.obj` matched `hello/full/obj/hello.obj` and
  `hello/full/wavefront-object/hello.obj`. Three files, one commit, invisible.
- Why nothing caught it, which is the interesting part: `generate_all_hello.py`
  writes the file, `hello/manifest.json` lists it, `audit_repo.py` finds it on
  disk, and all 19 acceptance checks pass. Five independent checks agree, and
  every one of them reads the working tree rather than the repository. A file
  can satisfy all of them and be in no clone anywhere.
- Scale: four files, once the fix was tested. `Gemfile.lock`, added while fixing
  the first, matched `hello/core/gemfile-lock/Gemfile.lock` -- and a
  `!hello/...` negation cannot undo it, because gitignore cannot re-include a
  file whose parent directory was excluded by an earlier rule.
- Fix: no bare extension patterns for anything a language could use; Ruby bundle
  files are matched by anchored paths instead.
- Regression guard: `tests/test_gitignore_safety.py`. The assertion that matters
  is `check-ignore --no-index`, which asks whether a file would be skipped if it
  were deleted and regenerated -- exactly what happens to the next contributor
  who adds a language with a colliding extension. Plain `check-ignore` and
  `git ls-files` both ignore tracked files, so they report a clean bill of
  health on a repository that is quietly wrong.
- Verified by re-adding `*.obj` and confirming the suite fails, then removing it
  and confirming it passes.
- Status: RESOLVED.


## E31 The show was crashing on every recorded run
- Symptom: the published GIF's final 22 seconds were a still frame. Three commits
  were spent trying to explain that as an animation-pacing problem before anyone
  looked at a frame of the video instead of a statistic about it.
- Cause: `run.sh` line 279 read `$FAST`, which nothing ever assigned. The script
  runs under `set -u`, so the line terminated it. The published GIF contains, in
  plain sight at t=16s:
      run.sh: line 279: FAST: unbound variable
- Why nothing local caught it, which is the whole lesson: the line sits behind
  `[ -t 1 ]`. Every local invocation pipes stdout, so every local run took the
  other branch and every local run succeeded. Only the recorder -- which gives
  run.sh a real pty -- ever executed it. `bash -n` parses the file and cannot see
  an unbound name. The script exited with an error rather than hanging, so no
  timeout fired either.
- Consequence: every phase after line 279 never ran. The "finale" visible in the
  recording was the art reveal from an earlier phase, held, which is why it
  measured as 94 distinct colours and a plausible-looking still image.
- Fix: assign `FAST`; make `--fast` set it, so the mode is real instead of dead.
  `banner_line()` read an identically undefined `$HUE` and had no callers, so it
  is deleted rather than fixed.
- Regression guard: `tests/test_shell_variables.py`. Every `$NAME` and `${NAME...}`
  run.sh mentions, comment-stripped with a quote-aware scanner and `read`/loop
  targets accounted for, must be assigned somewhere in the same file. Run against
  the previous run.sh it reports `['FAST', 'HUE']`; against the fixed one,
  nothing.
- Two mistakes worth recording alongside the bug:
  - I blamed `palettegen=max_colors=96` for flattening the gradient, and changed
    the encoder twice, before checking whether the source was animated at all.
    The encoder was innocent. The comfortable move was the wrong one: a codec
    setting is a number you can change, and the crash was a bug you had to find.
  - `measure_motion.py` reported "frozen" for 22 seconds and could not say why.
    Coverage tells you that nothing changed; only a picture tells you that
    something changed into an error message. The tool was not wrong, it was
    asked a question it does not answer, and I kept asking it that question
    three times.
- Status: RESOLVED.
